---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import CtaBand from "../components/CtaBand.astro";
---

<Base
	title="Our farm"
	description="Autism Sanctuary’s home on Pea Ridge Road: Edgefield, animals, gardens, Enchanted Forest and Spring Creek trails, and volunteer stewardship."
	image="/media/edgefield-aerial.jpg"
>
	<section class="page-banner">
		<div class="container">
			<span class="eyebrow">Pea Ridge Road · Western Albemarle</span>
			<h1>Our home—where the farm is the program.</h1>
			<p class="lede">
				Roughly 85 acres of trails, animals, gardens, and gathering spaces woven into Crozet, White Hall, and
				the wider Albemarle community. Licensed supports happen alongside mud, sunshine, and neighbors who know
				people by name.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section section-cream">
		<div class="container split">
			<div class="stack prose-wide">
				<h2>Why this place matters</h2>
				<p>
					The farm shows how a working landscape can still meet every DBHDS expectation: structured trails for
					movement and pre-vocational routines, gardens for therapeutic horticulture, barns and fields for
					animal care, and porches for rest and regulation—all staffed by DSPs who like being outside.
				</p>
				<ul class="checklist">
					<li>Days built for people with significant support needs</li>
					<li>Training that blends regulations with dirt, weather, and livestock</li>
					<li>Accessibility within a real working farm</li>
				</ul>
			</div>
			<figure class="media-frame">
				<img
					src="/media/edgefield-aerial.jpg"
					alt="Aerial view of Edgefield gardens and surrounding land."
					width="2560"
					height="1707"
					loading="lazy"
					decoding="async"
				/>
			</figure>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section">
		<div class="container">
			<div class="section-head">
				<span class="eyebrow">Animals &amp; agriculture</span>
				<h2>Cattle, chickens, gardens, and the high tunnel.</h2>
			</div>
			<div class="grid-3">
				<article class="feature-block">
					<h3>Cattle</h3>
					<p>
						Our cattle move regularly between pastures. Visitors often say hello through the fence; after a
						long, happy life, grass-fed cattle are processed into beef available through local partners and
						market tables when offered.
					</p>
				</article>
				<article class="feature-block">
					<h3>Chickens</h3>
					<p>
						Chickens roam fields in warmer weather and stay in the fenced garden in winter. Caring for them
						helps teach empathy—and their eggs appear at farmers market visits.
					</p>
				</article>
				<article class="feature-block">
					<h3>High tunnel &amp; gardens</h3>
					<p>
						Our 30&apos;×72&apos; high tunnel extends the growing season and anchors garden work, composting,
						and hands-on horticulture that people can return to week after week.
					</p>
				</article>
			</div>
			<figure class="media-frame" style="margin-top:2rem;">
				<img
					src="/media/farm-animals.jpg"
					alt="Animals and outdoor life on the Autism Sanctuary farm."
					width="1280"
					height="960"
					loading="lazy"
					decoding="async"
				/>
			</figure>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section section-meadow">
		<div class="container split reverse">
			<figure class="media-frame">
				<img
					src="/media/hero-enchanted-forest.jpg"
					alt="Trees along the Enchanted Forest trail."
					width="1976"
					height="2560"
					loading="lazy"
					decoding="async"
				/>
			</figure>
			<div class="stack prose-wide">
				<span class="eyebrow">Trails</span>
				<h2>Enchanted Forest &amp; Spring Creek</h2>
				<p>
					Immersing people in nature supports education, regulation, and development. We currently offer two
					walking trails on the property.
				</p>
				<article class="feature-block">
					<h3>Enchanted Forest Trail</h3>
					<p>
						A ¾-mile loop through forested land—more groomed and less sloped, with learning stations that
						promote skill building and creativity. Realized with support from CACF’s Enriching Communities
						Grant and volunteer partners.
					</p>
				</article>
				<article class="feature-block">
					<h3>Spring Creek Trail</h3>
					<p>
						A longer route that diverges downhill along Spring Creek. Steeper and more challenging, with a
						peaceful, engaging walk beside the water.
					</p>
				</article>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section section-cream">
		<div class="container split">
			<div class="stack prose-wide">
				<span class="eyebrow">Volunteer with us</span>
				<h2>Help keep the land ready for good days.</h2>
				<p>
					Volunteers support trail maintenance (raking, clearing debris, marking invasive species),
					agriculture in the high tunnel and gardens, and—with training and a weekly commitment—assistance
					alongside staff in the adult day program.
				</p>
				<p>
					Volunteer roles complement, never replace, paid DSP staffing. We match tasks to season, capacity,
					and safety.
				</p>
				<a class="btn btn-primary" href="/contact?intent=volunteer">Inquire about volunteering</a>
			</div>
			<figure class="media-frame">
				<img
					src="/media/sanctuary-gardens.jpg"
					alt="Garden beds and paths at Autism Sanctuary."
					width="2560"
					height="1920"
					loading="lazy"
					decoding="async"
				/>
			</figure>
		</div>
	</section>

	<CtaBand
		title="Come see how the land shapes the day"
		primaryHref="/contact"
		primaryLabel="Contact us"
		secondaryHref="/donate"
		secondaryLabel="Support the farm"
	/>
</Base>
